# Installed and off: nothing starts the provider until it has an address to sign people in at.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who enabled or disabled this unit keeps that choice across every
# later version.

post_install() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  tks-auth is installed and OFF. Give it its one address in /etc/tks-auth/tks-auth.env:

      Auth__Issuer   the URL a browser signs in at, e.g. https://auth.example.com

  then enable it:

      systemctl enable --now tks-auth.service

  On its first start it generates the key every token is signed with, at
  /var/lib/tks-auth/session-signing.pem — never shipped by any package; back it up with the account
  store — and leaves the first administrator's one-time password, readable by nobody else, in:

      /var/lib/tks-auth/initial-admin-password

  An https issuer is served by tks-auth-serve.service, which issues the name's certificate through
  nginx and certbot and renders its vhost: enable it once the name resolves to this machine. Until
  the issuer is a URL the provider signs nobody in.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/tks-auth/tks-auth.env, merge it — new
  settings are declared there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

  Opening an account store written by an earlier version upgrades it in place, after copying it to
  accounts.db.pre-<version> beside it.

MSG
}
